===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|millepede||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|milliped}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-}}
From {{uder|en|la|mīllipeda||wood louse}}, from {{m|la|mīlle||thousand}} + {{m|la|pēs}}, {{m|la|pedis||foot}}, equivalent to {{af|en|milli-<id:thousand>|-pede}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɪləpid/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-millipede.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-millipede.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any of many [[elongated]] [[arthropod]]s, of the [[class]] {{taxfmt|Diplopoda|class}}, with [[cylindrical]] [[bodies]] that have two [[pair]]s of [[leg]]s for each one of their 20 to 100 or more body [[segment]]s. {{defdate|from 1601}}
#: {{syn|en|diplopod}}
#* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=2|book=XX|chapter=Of the Serpentine Cucumber, called otherwise the Wandering Cucumber: also of the Garden Cucumbers, Melons or Pompions|page=37|passage=The ſaid leaves alſo ſtamped with honey and brought to the forme of a cataplaſme, cure the bloudie-falls or night-blains: but tempered with wine, they heale the bitings of dogs, as alſo of the '''Millepeed''', which the Greeks call Seps, a long worm with hairie feet, doing much harme to cattaile eſpecially; for looke where it biteth, the place preſently ſwelleth and putrifieth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=Rod Preston-Mafham|title=The Encyclopedia of Land Invertebrate Behaviour|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=sDpagGxJw3AC&pg=PA14&dq=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=LDiMU8LqB8HEkgWkqoGICw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&f=false|page=14
|passage=With one or two exceptions, male '''millipedes''' make direct contact with the female and transfer sperm via a pair of modified front legs (gonopods) which have been charged with semen from the genitalia situated towards the rear of the body.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Thomas Eisner|title=For Love of Insects|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Ki9djoKOm-0C&pg=PA276&dq=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=LDiMU8LqB8HEkgWkqoGICw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&f=false|page=276
|passage=When alive, '''millipedes''' maintain tension in these<sup>[intersegmental]</sup> muscles and are therefore difficult to straighten out when coiled.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Boris A. Byzov|chapter=4: Intestinal Microbiota of '''Millipedes'''|editors=Helmut König; Ajit Varma|title=Intestinal Microorganisms of Termites and Other Invertebrates|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=sbBG4-G7arQC&pg=PA89&dq=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=LDiMU8LqB8HEkgWkqoGICw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22millipede%22%7C%22millipedes%22&f=false|page=89
|passage=Soil '''millipedes''' (Diplopoda) possess a specific gut microbiota that differs from microbial communities in soil and leaf litter.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Allen Kim|title=A parasite that feeds off of the reproductive organs of millipedes is named after Twitter, where it was found|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2020/05/19/us/twitter-fungal-parasite-scn-trnd/index.html|date=2020-05-19|passage=Working with her colleague Henrik Enghoff, the two discovered several specimens of the same fungus on North American '''millipedes''' right there in the Natural History Museum’s collection.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Megan Marples|title=Taylor Swift inspired an entomologist to name a new millipede species after the megastar|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/20/us/taylor-swift-millipede-new-species-scn/index.html|date=2022-04-20|passage=An entomologist named a new species of '''millipede''' Nannaria swiftae after pop star Taylor Swift.}}

====Usage notes====
* The spelling "[[milliped]]" was formerly considered the only correct form by [[prescriptivist]]s and is still preferred by many scientists who work with [[myriapod]]s, but ''millipede'' is by far the most common spelling in actual use.

